def dc-motor-drv8833
  title "DC motor driver DRV8833: two motors (or one stepper) with its VM, VCP and VINT capacitors"
  about "A TI DRV8833 (HTSSOP-16 with PowerPAD) drives two brushed DC motors from VM 2.7–10.8 V, up to 1.5 A RMS and 2
      A peak per bridge (datasheet SLVSAR1E at VM 5 V, 25 °C), each on a JST XH connector. As the datasheet asks: a 10
      µF-class ceramic at VM, 10 nF from VCP to VM, 2.2 µF at VINT; a 100 µF electrolytic as the motor supply's local
      bulk capacitor. A 10 kΩ pull-down holds nSLEEP low – bridges off, inputs ignored – until the firmware drives it
      high; nFAULT has its pull-up. The bridge outputs clamp the windings with their own body diodes (clamp internal):
      no flyback diodes. Sense pins to ground: no current chopping, the overcurrent protection stays."
  port vm "the motor supply, 2.7–10.8 V (11.8 V absolute maximum)"
  port vcc "the logic supply of the MCU (3.3 V or 5 V) – nFAULT's pull-up"
  port sleep "nSLEEP from an MCU output: high = run, low = sleep (bridges off)"
  port ain1 "bridge A input 1 (MCU output, PWM)"
  port ain2 "bridge A input 2 (MCU output, PWM)"
  port bin1 "bridge B input 1 (MCU output, PWM)"
  port bin2 "bridge B input 2 (MCU output, PWM)"
  port fault "nFAULT to an MCU input: low on overcurrent, overtemperature or undervoltage"
  port gnd "ground"
  part U1 ic DRV8833PWPR lcsc C50506 "{as}: dual H-bridge DRV8833"
    notes "PowerPAD (pin 17) and GND (13) to ground; thermal vias from the pad to a ground plane (datasheet 10.1.1)."
  part C1 capacitor CL21A226MAQNNNE lcsc C45783 value 22uF "{as}: VM ceramic 22 µF"
    notes "Datasheet (pin functions, 10.1): a low-ESR ceramic of 10 µF minimum, rated for VM, close to the VM pin. 22
      µF because an X5R keeps only part of its value under DC bias; 25 V for VM up to 10.8 V (edakit's margin × 1.5:
      16.2 V)."
  part C2 capacitor RVT100UF25V67RV0011 lcsc C2836443 value 100uF "{as}: VM bulk 100 µF electrolytic"
    notes "Local bulk capacitance (datasheet 9.1: the size is left to system-level testing; figure 7 shows a polarized
      10 µF at VM). 100 µF is a design choice for the start and stall pulses of small motors; 25 V above VM, as 9.1
      asks for the energy a motor returns."
  part C3 capacitor 0603B103K500NT lcsc C57112 value 10nF "{as}: charge pump 10 nF VCP to VM"
    notes "Datasheet (pin functions, 10.1): 0.01 µF X7R rated 16 V minimum from VCP to VM, not to ground; this one 50
      V."
  part C4 capacitor CL10A225KO8NNNC lcsc C23630 value 2.2uF "{as}: VINT 2.2 µF"
    notes "Datasheet (pin functions, 10.1): 2.2 µF ceramic rated 6.3 V from VINT to ground; this one 16 V."
  part R1 resistor 0805W8F1002T5E lcsc C17414 value 10k "{as}: nSLEEP pull-down 10 k"
    notes "Holds nSLEEP low while the MCU pin floats (reset, boot): bridges off, charge pump stopped, inputs ignored
      (datasheet 7.3.4). Firmer than the internal 500 kΩ; an output at 3.3 V drives 0.33 mA into it."
  part R2 resistor 0805W8F1002T5E lcsc C17414 value 10k "{as}: nFAULT pull-up 10 k"
    notes "nFAULT is open drain, VOL ≤ 0.5 V at 5 mA (datasheet 6.5); 10 kΩ from 3.3 V or 5 V draws well below that."
  part J1 connector B2B-XH-A(LF)(SN) lcsc C158012 "{as}: motor A (JST XH, 1 = AOUT1)"
    notes "JST XH, 3 A with AWG 22 wire: above the bridge's 2 A peak. Another family than the JST PH supply input
      (block power-input-jst), so that a supply plug does not fit."
  part J2 connector B2B-XH-A(LF)(SN) lcsc C158012 "{as}: motor B (JST XH, 1 = BOUT1)"
  net vm 0..10.8 V U1.VM C1.1 C2.plus C3.1
  net vcc R2.2
  net sleep @vcc U1.nSleep R1.1
  net ain1 @vcc U1.AIN1
  net ain2 @vcc U1.AIN2
  net bin1 @vcc U1.BIN1
  net bin2 @vcc U1.BIN2
  net fault @vcc U1.nFault R2.1
  net vcp 0..17.1 V U1.VCP C3.2
    notes "Charge pump above VM; the datasheet gives no VCP voltage – 10.8 V (VM max) + 6.3 V (the rating asked of the
      VINT capacitor) as an assumed upper bound."
  net vint 0..6.3 V U1.VINT C4.1
    notes "Internal supply; the datasheet gives no voltage – 6.3 V, the rating it asks of the VINT capacitor, as the
      upper bound."
  net aout1 @vm U1.AOUT1 J1.1
  net aout2 @vm U1.AOUT2 J1.2
  net bout1 @vm U1.BOUT1 J2.1
  net bout2 @vm U1.BOUT2 J2.2
  net gnd U1.GND U1.AISEN U1.BISEN C1.2 C2.minus C4.2 R1.2
  covers REQ.missing LVL.undefined LIM.v HINT.motor_supply
  notes "VM 2.7–10.8 V (recommended operating conditions; 11.8 V absolute maximum – a full 3-cell LiPo is above it).
      The motor supply needs no reverse-polarity diode in series: behind one the energy a braking motor returns lifts
      VM (HINT.motor_supply); protect the input with a P-MOSFET (block reverse-pmos) or a clamp behind the diode."
  notes "Bridge logic (datasheet table 1): xIN1 = xIN2 = 0 coast (outputs Z), 1/0 forward, 0/1 reverse, 1/1 brake. PWM
      on one input with the other low = fast decay, with the other high = slow decay (table 2). VIH 2 V, VIL 0.7 V,
      inputs up to 5.75 V: 3.3 V and 5 V logic drive them; nSLEEP needs 2.5 V. Up to 1 ms after nSLEEP goes high
      before the bridges switch (tWAKE)."
  notes "While the firmware starts: R1 keeps the driver asleep, and the bridge inputs have internal 150 kΩ pull-downs
      (coast). Where the firmware never needs sleep, nSLEEP may go to VM through 20–75 kΩ instead of R1 and the MCU
      pin (datasheet 7.3.4: the pin clamps at 6.5 V, at most 250 µA into it)."
  notes "AISEN and BISEN go to ground: no current chopping (7.3.3). To limit start and stall current, a sense resistor
      per bridge, R = 0.2 V / ICHOP (VTRIP 160–240 mV), rated for I²R. The overcurrent protection (IOCP 2–3.3 A, retry
      after 1.35 ms) works without them."
  notes "The outputs carry clamp internal – the body diodes take the winding current in fast decay (7.3.2):
      REQ.missing asks for no flyback diode, and a single diode across a winding driven both ways would short the
      bridge."
  notes "edakit check warns LVL.undefined/open_drain on the motor nets (the outputs are tristate): Z in coast or
      sleep, where the motor is meant to run free – a bridge output, no logic line. HINT.motor_supply appears once a
      motor (kind motor) is in the graph."
  notes "Unused inputs to ground (coast) – edakit wants two pins on every net (STRUCT.net), as the block's test does
      with AIN2, BIN1 and BIN2. The two bridges may be paralleled for one motor of up to 3 A RMS (datasheet 8.2,
      figure 7): AIN1 with BIN1, AIN2 with BIN2, AOUT1 with BOUT1, AOUT2 with BOUT2."
  notes "sim.json: the driving nets under the MCU's outputs, a motor at J1 as an `external` load of its running
      current. The DRV8833's behaviour model (bridge) drives the outputs by the truth table, each side through its on
      resistance (0.325 Ω high, 0.275 Ω low, max at 5 V and 85 °C): edakit sim checks each output's current against
      1.5 A and the junction temperature, not the stall current V / R_winding against the overcurrent trip, nor the
      bulk capacitor's ripple rating (93 mA rms at 120 Hz; the ceramic C1 is meant to carry the PWM ripple) – a motor
      is a constant current in the operating point, its stall and PWM are the review's."
  set test {
      "connect": {
        "vm": "+5V",
        "vcc": "3V3",
        "sleep": "SIG",
        "ain1": "SIG2",
        "ain2": "GND",
        "bin1": "GND",
        "bin2": "GND"},
      "sim": {
        "mcu": {
          "U1": {
            "outputs": ["LED_SDI", "LED_CLK", "LED_LE", "LED_OE", "SIG", "SIG2"],
            "pullups": ["BTN"],
            "pullup_ohm": 50000}},
        "loads": {
          "t_run": {
            "about": "the driver awake (nSLEEP high), bridge A forward into a motor at J2 drawing 0.2 A (1 → 2) – within the example's 0.5 A USB source",
            "gpio": {"LED_OE": 1, "SIG": 1, "SIG2": 1},
            "leds_on": [],
            "external": {"J2": {"plus": "1", "minus": "2", "i_a": 0.2}}}}}}
